Z||000000||phillip||04-26-2000||01:35 AM||||<BR>thinking about the pylon python fiero that uses the pre northstar cadillac aluminum v8. seems like it would be a good swap candidate, but not much is said about it by ace or v8 archie or mentioned in kit car mag. articles. any one know why? any one heard of any other fieros with one. i only know of one short article where one was put in a froht wheel drive monte carlo (short blurb in a hot rod type mag).<P>just exploring all the options. i'd sure like to lose my 4cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||04-26-2000||11:02 AM||||Seems like the 4.9 only makes about 200 HP. (Anybody?) <BR>If that's the case, there are probably some more common V-6 swaps that will make as much.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||04-26-2000||11:27 AM||||According to the web site: (http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/4_9Lengine.html)  yes, the 4.9L only makes 200 HP at 4400 RPM. But, it makes 275 ft-lbs at 3000 RPMs. This torque number is the same for the Gen II 3800 SC. With such low HP numbers at high RPMs and high torque numbers at low RPMs, it just means that the motor make most of it power low RPMs. <P>These are great street numbers and would mean a very quick car but one without a lot of top end. You could use an "economy" gear ratio and plant the tires shift early to stay in the power band and probably take just about any car off the line from 0-60. After that, the motor just runs out of air. Just remember: Torque gets you off the line, horsepower gets you speed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000003||kevin||04-27-2000||01:51 AM||||Remember guts and a keen driving skill will put your car ahead of the competition. Understand there are lots of guys with big wallets but small brains. Better to have an understanding of what your car can do at the limit rather than shut down before the race concludes. Fisherman always brag about the big fish that got away. My point: learn your Fiero's strengths, capitalize on them, learn from your wins and defeats, but do not brag about having tremendous horsepower if you can't use it. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||04-27-2000||12:16 PM||||Roy, Thanks for the info. I had no idea the torque figures were that high. <BR>Sounds like it would make a good stump-puller.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>A couple of weeks ago, someone on this forum had (what turned out to be) a 4.9 lined up through one of his local "recyclers." (The recycler tried to tell him it was a Northstar, though. He may have changed his mind.) <P>Steve<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 04-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FieroGTRacingInc||04-27-2000||01:43 PM||||No... It was a 4.5L, not a 4.9L....  It was me. The man had told me that IT WAS A NORTHSTAR, and it ended up not being a N* at all... So, I passed on it AND GOT MY MONEY BACK that I had previously paid him  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>                  1FieroGTRacingInc.||||reg||1
Z||000006||spike||04-29-2000||10:13 AM||||It sounds like this would be a better option over the 350 V8 engine.  The 4.9l is Aluminum wright.  It must be alot lighter then that big old 350 cast iron block.  I would imagine that you can find a 4.9l with its matted tranny and mount it in the Fiero Longitudally.  The prices for these engines are good and can be had quite cheaply.  I would imagine that no kit would have to be purchased.  The installation would reguire some custom parts and maybe the loss of the trunk wich is not a big deal to me.  Would you say that the wiring would cause problems.<BR> Just the amount of money being saved on the swap kit would help out alot.  What do you guys really think about going this route.  Any idea how hard it would be to complete verses a 350 V8 swap.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||phillip||04-29-2000||12:06 PM||||i thought it was a front wheel drive engine too. i was thinking it would go in easier than a small block since it was. though maybe not as easy as a 6. if i could i'd like to turn the engine around right ( assuming i could do it at all) but it's harder to do if you want a manual tranny. i wonder if a c-5 vette tranny would be adaptable since it is part of the rear end now.just kind of put the engine and tranny back together without the connecting shaft.||||reg||1
